const VICTORIAN_DATA = {"lessons":[{"id":1,"title":"The Victorian Age","kicker":"1837–1901 · Change & contradiction","intro":"The Victorian Age takes its name from Queen Victoria. Industrial growth, larger cities and a growing middle class changed Britain. Progress did not remove poverty. Novelists explored the gap between public respectability and lived experience.","german":"Industrialisierung brachte Fortschritt, aber auch soziale Gegensätze. Verbinde historischen Kontext und literarische Darstellung; eine Epoche ist keine Garantie, dass jeder Roman gleich aussieht.","sections":[{"title":"An age of change","text":"Queen Victoria reigned from 1837 to 1901. Industrialisation expanded factory production and transformed work. Urbanisation means the growth of towns and cities.","german":"Die Industrielle Revolution begann bereits vor Victorias Regierungszeit und setzte sich im 19. Jahrhundert fort.","example":"London offers Pip new opportunities, but his new surroundings do not automatically make him morally better."},{"title":"Readers and social class","text":"The expanding middle class became an important audience for novels. Writers entertained readers while examining inequality, ambition and social responsibility.","german":"Die Mittelschicht war ein wichtiges Publikum, aber nicht die einzige Leserschaft. Gesellschaftskritik und Unterhaltung konnten zusammenwirken.","example":"Pip links being a gentleman with social advancement; the story questions this definition of personal worth."},{"title":"Publication is not setting","text":"Great Expectations was published in 1860–1861. Much of its action is set earlier in the nineteenth century. A Victorian novel need not set every scene during Victoria’s reign.","german":"Unterscheide Erscheinungszeit, erzählte Zeit und Erzählzeit.","example":"Call the book Victorian because of its publication context; do not claim Pip’s entire childhood occurs after 1837."}]},{"id":2,"title":"The Publishing World","kicker":"Readers · Markets · Suspense","intro":"Fiction reached readers through books, periodicals and circulating libraries. Serial publication divided a story into instalments. Writers needed to sustain interest across pauses, so questions, turning points and revelations could shape the rhythm of a novel.","german":"Die Publikationsform beeinflusst die Erzählweise. Eine Fortsetzung muss Erinnerungen an frühere Teile wecken und Neugier auf den nächsten Teil erzeugen.","sections":[{"title":"Access to fiction","text":"Circulating libraries lent books, commonly for a subscription. Periodicals were publications issued regularly and could include fiction as well as other material.","german":"Leihbibliotheken verleihen Bücher; Zeitschriften erscheinen regelmässig. Das sind zwei unterschiedliche Zugangswege.","example":"A reader could borrow a novel rather than buy an expensive complete edition."},{"title":"A story in parts","text":"An instalment is one part of a serial. Suspense makes readers want to know what will happen next; a cliffhanger leaves an important situation unresolved.","german":"Nicht jede Fortsetzung endet mit einem Cliffhanger. Die Form begünstigt aber Episoden, Wendepunkte und Spannungsbögen.","example":"Magwitch’s revelation changes the meaning of Pip’s earlier hopes and renews curiosity about his future."},{"title":"Readers, writers and reviewers","text":"Reviews and readers’ reactions influenced literary reputations and the market. Serial form could allow responses during publication, but reader influence did not mean complete control over every story.","german":"Öffentliche Reaktionen wirkten auf Erfolg und Erwartungen; sie bestimmen nicht automatisch jeden Ausgang.","example":"Great Expectations appeared in the weekly periodical All the Year Round in 1860–1861."}]},{"id":3,"title":"Features of Victorian Novels","kicker":"Realism · Responsibility · Voice","intro":"Many Victorian novels combine recognisable social worlds with moral questions. Realism, social criticism and didacticism describe different aspects of a text. They can work alongside humour or Gothic effects rather than excluding them.","german":"Unterscheide Darstellung, Kritik und moralische Absicht. Vermeide die Behauptung, jeder viktorianische Roman verwende dieselbe Erzählperspektive.","sections":[{"title":"Three useful terms","text":"Realism creates a recognisable social world. Social criticism questions injustice or harmful values. Didacticism aims to teach or guide a reader morally.","german":"Realismus betrifft die Darstellung; Sozialkritik hinterfragt Missstände; Didaktik bezeichnet eine belehrende Absicht.","example":"Pip’s embarrassment about Joe makes class prejudice visible and invites criticism of it."},{"title":"Characters and settings","text":"Characters can have mixed motives and develop through mistakes. Urban settings can expose inequality, anonymity and ambition. Rural places are not automatically morally perfect.","german":"Realistische Figuren können widersprüchlich sein. Ein Handlungsort ist kein Beweis für den moralischen Wert aller Menschen dort.","example":"London expands Pip’s social world, while Joe’s kindness questions Pip’s judgement of his rural background."},{"title":"Narrator: a crucial exception","text":"An omniscient narrator can know more than any single character. Great Expectations instead uses retrospective first-person narration: adult Pip tells and evaluates his younger self’s story.","german":"Pip ist ein rückblickender Ich-Erzähler, kein allwissender Erzähler. Erfahrung und spätere Einsicht stehen in Spannung.","example":"The older narrator can reveal the mistakes of the younger Pip without knowing every other mind directly."}]},{"id":4,"title":"Types of Victorian Fiction","kicker":"Categories that can overlap","intro":"The lesson sheet groups Victorian writing into the novel of manners, the humanitarian novel, the Bildungsroman and literary nonsense. These are useful teaching categories, not four boxes that exclude each other. Literary nonsense is wider than the novel: Lear’s verse is an important example.","german":"Ein Roman kann mehreren Typen angehören. Literary nonsense ist eine literarische Richtung und umfasst auch Gedichte; Edward Lear ist kein notwendiges Beispiel für einen Romanautor.","sections":[{"title":"Manners and humanity","text":"A novel of manners examines social codes, rank and behaviour; Thackeray’s Vanity Fair is a useful example. Humanitarian fiction encourages sympathy for vulnerable people and questions injustice; Dickens is a major representative.","german":"Sittenroman: Gesellschaftsregeln und soziale Rollen. Humanitärer Roman: Mitgefühl und soziale Verantwortung.","example":"Great Expectations asks readers to reconsider the moral value of a poor blacksmith and a convicted man."},{"title":"Formation and development","text":"A Bildungsroman follows the formation of a central character’s identity through youth, experience and learning. Dickens’s Great Expectations and Charlotte Brontë’s Jane Eyre offer examples.","german":"Bildungsroman: Die innere Entwicklung zählt, nicht bloss die Tatsache, dass die Figur älter wird.","example":"Pip changes his understanding of what makes someone a gentleman."},{"title":"Nonsense and categories","text":"Literary nonsense plays with language, logic and social rules. Lewis Carroll’s Alice’s Adventures in Wonderland and Edward Lear’s A Book of Nonsense belong here.","german":"Nonsense ist kunstvoll gestaltet, nicht einfach schlecht verständliche Literatur. Lear arbeitet besonders mit Versen.","example":"A nonsense world challenges ordinary logic; Pip’s moral growth is better explained through formation and social criticism."}]},{"id":5,"title":"Characters & Social Values","kicker":"Pip · Joe · Estella · Havisham · Magwitch","intro":"Characters are more than facts to memorise. Ask what each relationship reveals about social class, identity and moral judgement. Pip’s changing responses to Joe and Magwitch make the difference between status and personal worth especially clear.","german":"Figurenanalyse verbindet Handlungen, Beziehungen und Themen. Frage nicht nur, wer jemand ist, sondern welche Wertvorstellung eine Beziehung sichtbar macht.","sections":[{"title":"Pip and Joe","text":"Pip’s ambition makes him ashamed of his origins and of Joe’s manners. Joe’s honesty and loyalty expose the weakness of treating social polish as moral superiority.","german":"Joe ist mit Pips Schwester verheiratet, also Pips Schwager. Pips spätere Reue ist ein Teil seiner Entwicklung.","example":"Contrast Pip’s embarrassment about Joe with Joe’s continuing kindness."},{"title":"Estella and Miss Havisham","text":"Pip’s attraction to Estella is entangled with his shame about class. Miss Havisham’s wealthy world encourages mistaken expectations; Pip wrongly assumes that she is his benefactor.","german":"Estella ist Miss Havishams Adoptivtochter. Liebe, Adoption und finanzielle Unterstützung sind verschiedene Beziehungen.","example":"Pip loves Estella, but that does not establish who pays for his life in London."},{"title":"Magwitch and prejudice","text":"Magwitch is an escaped convict and Pip’s real benefactor. His financial support challenges the idea that a low social reputation reveals everything about a person’s humanity.","german":"Seine strafrechtliche Stellung und seine Menschlichkeit müssen unterschieden werden. Geld allein macht auch ihn nicht moralisch vollkommen.","example":"Pip must reconsider a man he first encountered with fear rather than social respect."}]},{"id":6,"title":"Literary Analysis","kicker":"Make a claim · Use evidence · Explain","intro":"A useful analysis connects an event with a literary idea and explains its effect. Calling Pip’s story a Bildungsroman is only the start. Show what changes, how narration shapes our judgement, and why class and kindness matter to that change.","german":"Eine Prüfungsantwort braucht Behauptung, Beispiel und Erklärung. Inhalt nacherzählen allein ist noch keine Analyse.","sections":[{"title":"Development and compassion","text":"Pip’s movement from shame towards regret gives his story a moral structure. The reader is invited to sympathise with people whom social prejudice can dismiss.","german":"Bildungsroman und humanitärer Roman verbinden Entwicklung und Mitgefühl.","example":"Joe’s loyalty and Magwitch’s support reveal the limits of Pip’s early class judgements."},{"title":"Realism and Gothic atmosphere","text":"Dickens combines a recognisable social world with mystery, decay and disturbing spaces. Miss Havisham’s house creates a Gothic atmosphere that can externalise a damaged relation to the past.","german":"Gothic elements: Geheimnis, Verfall und unheimliche Atmosphäre. Eine Deutung der Wirkung muss als Interpretation begründet werden.","example":"The house can be read as a contrast to Pip’s hopes of a fresh, successful future."},{"title":"Place and perspective","text":"The forge and the rural world contrast with London’s opportunities and social pressures. Adult Pip’s retrospective voice makes his earlier errors available for critical reflection.","german":"Weder Land noch Stadt sind automatisch moralisch eindeutig. Erzählperspektive erklärt, wie wir Pips Fehler wahrnehmen.","example":"Compare where Pip seeks status with the people whose loyalty he later recognises."}]},{"id":7,"title":"Women Writers","kicker":"Authorship · Readers · Recognition","intro":"Women were major readers and writers of Victorian fiction. Charlotte and Emily Brontë and George Eliot shaped the literary period. Social expectations and prejudice could restrict professional opportunities, while pseudonyms offered some writers a way to negotiate those pressures.","german":"Frauen waren nicht bloss Publikum. Sie prägten den Roman aktiv. Manche verwendeten Pseudonyme; das gilt nicht für jede Schriftstellerin und hatte nicht überall dieselben Gründe.","sections":[{"title":"Important voices","text":"Charlotte Brontë wrote Jane Eyre; Emily Brontë wrote Wuthering Heights. George Eliot, whose name was Mary Ann Evans, wrote novels including Middlemarch.","german":"Verwechsle die Brontë-Schwestern nicht. George Eliot ist ein literarischer Name, kein männlicher Schriftsteller in dieser Liste.","example":"Jane Eyre offers a useful point of comparison with a narrative of identity and development."},{"title":"Names and prejudice","text":"A pen name is a name used for publication. The Brontës published as Currer, Ellis and Acton Bell; these names did not openly identify them as women. George Eliot used a masculine literary name.","german":"Nicht alle Pseudonyme waren eindeutig männlich: Die Bell-Vornamen waren bewusst geschlechtlich nicht eindeutig.","example":"Analyse how a name might affect expectations about an author without assuming a pseudonym removed every barrier."},{"title":"Readers and institutions","text":"Women’s work, reading and purchasing helped shape literary culture. Gender expectations could limit education, professional recognition and access to publication; experiences varied.","german":"Gesellschaftliche Schranken und literarischer Erfolg existierten gleichzeitig. Keine pauschale Behauptung, Frauen hätten nie publizieren können.","example":"An exam answer can connect the growth of novel reading with the active contribution of women writers."}]},{"id":8,"title":"Exam Challenge","kicker":"From recall to an argued answer","intro":"Bring the historical context, the publishing world and literary analysis together. A strong exam response makes a precise claim, supports it with a relevant example and explains the relationship. Use the toolbox for language support, then try without it.","german":"Keine scheinbare KI-Bewertung: Freie Antworten werden gespeichert und anhand von Musterantwort und Kriterien selbst überprüft. Übe mit mehreren Themen statt nur mit Handlungserinnerung.","sections":[{"title":"Plan before you write","text":"Identify the task: define, compare, explain or evaluate. Select two or three relevant ideas and an example for each. Avoid a list of labels with no argument.","german":"Aufgabenverb beachten; eine Frage nach Wirkung verlangt mehr als eine Definition.","example":"For a question about class, combine Pip’s shame, Joe’s loyalty and the revelation about Magwitch."},{"title":"Use evidence carefully","text":"Paraphrase relevant events accurately. Do not invent quotations or chapter numbers for an abridged edition. A claim about a general pattern should allow exceptions.","german":"Originalroman und gekürzte Ausgabe können sich unterscheiden. Die Lernumgebung verwendet den Lernzettel und überprüfte Kontextinformationen.","example":"Name retrospective first-person narration as an exception to a common Victorian narrative pattern."},{"title":"Check your own answer","text":"Check accuracy, an explicit link between context and novel, concrete examples and clear English. Use cautious language for interpretations and precise terms for facts.","german":"Selbstkontrolle ist transparent: fachliche Korrektheit, Belege, Erklärung und Sprache.","example":"Write “can be read as” for an interpretation of the house, rather than presenting one possible meaning as the only fact."}]}],"flashcards":[{"id":"c1","sourceId":1,"level":1,"category":"The Victorian Age","front":"When was the Victorian Age?","back":"1837–1901, during the reign of Queen Victoria.","question":"When was the Victorian Age?","answer":"1837–1901, during the reign of Queen Victoria.","german":"Queen Victoria regierte von 1837 bis 1901. Die Industrialisierung begann bereits früher.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c2","sourceId":2,"level":1,"category":"The Victorian Age","front":"How did industrialisation change society?","back":"Cities grew, and social inequality remained a major problem.","question":"How did industrialisation change society?","answer":"Cities grew, and social inequality remained a major problem.","german":"Die Industrialisierung liess Städte wachsen; soziale Ungleichheit blieb bestehen.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c3","sourceId":3,"level":1,"category":"The Victorian Age","front":"Why did the readership grow?","back":"The middle class expanded and became an important readership.","question":"Why did the readership grow?","answer":"The middle class expanded and became an important readership.","german":"Die wachsende Mittelschicht war ein wichtiges, aber nicht das einzige Lesepublikum.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c4","sourceId":4,"level":2,"category":"The Victorian Age","front":"What were circulating libraries?","back":"Libraries that allowed people to borrow books.","question":"What were circulating libraries?","answer":"Libraries that allowed people to borrow books.","german":"Circulating libraries sind Leihbibliotheken. Bücher mussten nicht einzeln gekauft werden.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c5","sourceId":5,"level":1,"category":"The Victorian Age","front":"What was the most popular literary form?","back":"The novel became the main form of literary entertainment.","question":"What was the most popular literary form?","answer":"The novel became the main form of literary entertainment.","german":"Der Roman wurde zu einer führenden Form literarischer Unterhaltung.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c6","sourceId":6,"level":2,"category":"The Victorian Age","front":"What is serial publication?","back":"Publishing a novel in separate instalments in periodicals.","question":"What is serial publication?","answer":"Publishing a novel in separate instalments in periodicals.","german":"Serial publication bedeutet Veröffentlichung in einzelnen Fortsetzungsteilen, häufig in Zeitschriften.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c7","sourceId":7,"level":2,"category":"The Victorian Age","front":"Why did writers need gripping stories?","back":"They had to keep readers interested in buying the next instalment.","question":"Why did writers need gripping stories?","answer":"They had to keep readers interested in buying the next instalment.","german":"Spannung und offene Fragen sollten Neugier auf die nächste Fortsetzung wecken.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c8","sourceId":8,"level":2,"category":"The Victorian Age","front":"What influence did reviewers have?","back":"They influenced public opinion and the reception of novels.","question":"What influence did reviewers have?","answer":"They influenced public opinion and the reception of novels.","german":"Rezensenten beeinflussten die öffentliche Wahrnehmung, kontrollierten aber nicht jede Meinung.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c9","sourceId":9,"level":3,"category":"Features","front":"What is realism?","back":"The portrayal of everyday life and believable characters.","question":"What is realism?","answer":"The portrayal of everyday life and believable characters.","german":"Realismus stellt Alltagsleben und glaubwürdige Figuren dar. Er schliesst Gothic elements nicht aus.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c10","sourceId":10,"level":3,"category":"Features","front":"What is social criticism?","back":"The criticism of social problems and inequalities.","question":"What is social criticism?","answer":"The criticism of social problems and inequalities.","german":"Sozialkritik hinterfragt soziale Probleme und Ungleichheiten.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c11","sourceId":11,"level":3,"category":"Features","front":"What is didacticism?","back":"The use of literature to teach moral lessons.","question":"What is didacticism?","answer":"The use of literature to teach moral lessons.","german":"Didaktik bedeutet eine belehrende Absicht oder moralische Lernwirkung.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c12","sourceId":12,"level":3,"category":"Features","front":"What responsibility did Victorian novelists feel?","back":"They felt a moral and social responsibility to their readers.","question":"What responsibility did Victorian novelists feel?","answer":"They felt a moral and social responsibility to their readers.","german":"Viele Romanautoren empfanden Verantwortung gegenüber ihrem Publikum; das gilt nicht ausnahmslos.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c13","sourceId":13,"level":3,"category":"Features","front":"What is an omniscient narrator?","back":"An all-knowing narrator who knows the characters' thoughts and feelings.","question":"What is an omniscient narrator?","answer":"An all-knowing narrator who knows the characters' thoughts and feelings.","german":"Allwissendes Erzählen erschliesst Gedanken und Gefühle verschiedener Figuren. Pip erzählt dagegen als Ich.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c14","sourceId":14,"level":3,"category":"Features","front":"What moral distinction was typical?","back":"Narrators often distinguished between right and wrong behaviour.","question":"What moral distinction was typical?","answer":"Narrators often distinguished between right and wrong behaviour.","german":"Die Unterscheidung von richtigem und falschem Verhalten konnte moralisches Lernen anregen.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c15","sourceId":15,"level":3,"category":"Features","front":"What was the typical setting?","back":"The city, a symbol of industrial society and anonymity.","question":"What was the typical setting?","answer":"The city, a symbol of industrial society and anonymity.","german":"Die Stadt war ein häufiger Schauplatz, aber nicht die einzige mögliche Umgebung eines viktorianischen Romans.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c16","sourceId":16,"level":3,"category":"Features","front":"Why were realistic characters important?","back":"Readers could identify with them and their experiences.","question":"Why were realistic characters important?","answer":"Readers could identify with them and their experiences.","german":"Glaubwürdige Erfahrungen erleichtern Identifikation, ohne dass eine Figur immer sympathisch sein muss.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c17","sourceId":17,"level":4,"category":"Types","front":"What is a novel of manners?","back":"A novel about social conventions, class and economic problems.","question":"What is a novel of manners?","answer":"A novel about social conventions, class and economic problems.","german":"Der Sittenroman untersucht gesellschaftliche Regeln, Schichten und wirtschaftliche Beziehungen.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c18","sourceId":18,"level":4,"category":"Types","front":"Who represented the novel of manners?","back":"William Makepeace Thackeray.","question":"Who represented the novel of manners?","answer":"William Makepeace Thackeray.","german":"Thackeray ist ein wichtiger Vertreter; ein Beispiel ist Vanity Fair.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c19","sourceId":19,"level":4,"category":"Types","front":"What is a humanitarian novel?","back":"A novel combining social criticism with sympathy for disadvantaged people.","question":"What is a humanitarian novel?","answer":"A novel combining social criticism with sympathy for disadvantaged people.","german":"Humanitäre Romane verbinden Mitgefühl mit Sozialkritik.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c20","sourceId":20,"level":4,"category":"Types","front":"What is a Bildungsroman?","back":"A novel following a character's development from youth to maturity.","question":"What is a Bildungsroman?","answer":"A novel following a character's development from youth to maturity.","german":"Ein Bildungsroman zeigt innere Entwicklung; blosses Älterwerden genügt nicht.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c21","sourceId":21,"level":4,"category":"Types","front":"Who wrote important humanitarian novels?","back":"Charles Dickens.","question":"Who wrote important humanitarian novels?","answer":"Charles Dickens.","german":"Dickens ist ein wichtiger Vertreter des humanitären Romans.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c22","sourceId":22,"level":4,"category":"Types","front":"What is literary nonsense and who represented it?","back":"Absurd worlds without normal logic; Edward Lear and Lewis Carroll.","question":"What is literary nonsense and who represented it?","answer":"Absurd worlds without normal logic; Edward Lear and Lewis Carroll.","german":"Nonsense spielt mit gewohnter Logik und Sprache. Die Form ist absichtsvoll gestaltet; Lear schrieb insbesondere Verse.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c23","sourceId":23,"level":2,"category":"Great Expectations","front":"When was Great Expectations first published?","back":"In instalments in 1860–1861.","question":"When was Great Expectations first published?","answer":"In instalments in 1860–1861.","german":"Great Expectations erschien 1860–1861 in Fortsetzungen; Erscheinungszeit und Handlungszeit unterscheiden.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c24","sourceId":24,"level":6,"category":"Great Expectations","front":"Why is Great Expectations a Bildungsroman?","back":"It follows Pip's development from childhood to adulthood.","question":"Why is Great Expectations a Bildungsroman?","answer":"It follows Pip's development from childhood to adulthood.","german":"Pips Weg von Kindheit zur Reife umfasst eine veränderte moralische Haltung.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c25","sourceId":25,"level":5,"category":"Great Expectations","front":"What is Pip's background?","back":"He is a poor orphan who grows up near a blacksmith's forge.","question":"What is Pip's background?","answer":"He is a poor orphan who grows up near a blacksmith's forge.","german":"Pip ist ein armes Waisenkind und wächst in der ländlichen Welt der Schmiede auf.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c26","sourceId":26,"level":5,"category":"Great Expectations","front":"Why does Pip want to become a gentleman?","back":"He loves Estella and is ashamed of his social background.","question":"Why does Pip want to become a gentleman?","answer":"He loves Estella and is ashamed of his social background.","german":"Seine Liebe zu Estella verbindet sich mit Scham über die eigene Herkunft und sozialem Ehrgeiz.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c27","sourceId":27,"level":5,"category":"Great Expectations","front":"Why is Pip ashamed of Joe?","back":"Pip wrongly associates Joe's humble social position with inferiority.","question":"Why is Pip ashamed of Joe?","answer":"Pip wrongly associates Joe's humble social position with inferiority.","german":"Pips Abwertung Joes offenbart sein eigenes Klassenvorurteil, nicht Joes tatsächlichen Minderwert.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c28","sourceId":28,"level":5,"category":"Great Expectations","front":"What does Joe represent?","back":"Kindness, honesty and loyalty.","question":"What does Joe represent?","answer":"Kindness, honesty and loyalty.","german":"Joe steht für Güte, Ehrlichkeit und Loyalität.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c29","sourceId":29,"level":5,"category":"Great Expectations","front":"Who secretly finances Pip's rise?","back":"Magwitch, a convicted criminal.","question":"Who secretly finances Pip's rise?","answer":"Magwitch, a convicted criminal.","german":"Magwitch ist der geheime Wohltäter, nicht Miss Havisham.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c30","sourceId":30,"level":6,"category":"Great Expectations","front":"How does Dickens criticise social class?","back":"He shows that wealth and social status do not determine human worth.","question":"How does Dickens criticise social class?","answer":"He shows that wealth and social status do not determine human worth.","german":"Dickens trennt gesellschaftlichen Rang von menschlichem Wert.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c31","sourceId":31,"level":6,"category":"Great Expectations","front":"What is the novel's moral message?","back":"Kindness and loyalty are more important than wealth and status.","question":"What is the novel's moral message?","answer":"Kindness and loyalty are more important than wealth and status.","german":"Güte und Loyalität sind wichtiger als Reichtum oder Status.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c32","sourceId":32,"level":6,"category":"Great Expectations","front":"Why is it a humanitarian novel?","back":"It challenges social prejudice and shows sympathy for marginalised people.","question":"Why is it a humanitarian novel?","answer":"It challenges social prejudice and shows sympathy for marginalised people.","german":"Der Roman hinterfragt soziale Vorurteile und weckt Mitgefühl für ausgegrenzte Menschen.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c33","sourceId":33,"level":6,"category":"Great Expectations","front":"Who narrates Great Expectations?","back":"The adult Pip, looking back on his own life.","question":"Who narrates Great Expectations?","answer":"The adult Pip, looking back on his own life.","german":"Der erwachsene Pip blickt auf sein eigenes früheres Leben zurück.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c34","sourceId":34,"level":6,"category":"Great Expectations","front":"How does its narration differ from typical Victorian narration?","back":"It uses a retrospective first-person narrator rather than an omniscient narrator.","question":"How does its narration differ from typical Victorian narration?","answer":"It uses a retrospective first-person narrator rather than an omniscient narrator.","german":"Rückblickendes Ich-Erzählen unterscheidet sich vom allwissenden Erzähler vieler, aber nicht aller viktorianischen Romane.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c35","sourceId":35,"level":6,"category":"Great Expectations","front":"What settings does Dickens contrast?","back":"The rural world around the forge and the city of London.","question":"What settings does Dickens contrast?","answer":"The rural world around the forge and the city of London.","german":"Die Welt um die Schmiede kontrastiert mit London; kein Ort enthält ausschliesslich gute oder schlechte Menschen.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c36","sourceId":36,"level":6,"category":"Great Expectations","front":"How does Dickens create a Gothic atmosphere?","back":"Miss Havisham's decaying house creates mystery and a dark atmosphere.","question":"How does Dickens create a Gothic atmosphere?","answer":"Miss Havisham's decaying house creates mystery and a dark atmosphere.","german":"Verfall, Geheimnisse und düstere Atmosphäre machen Miss Havishams Haus unheimlich.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c37","sourceId":37,"level":7,"category":"Women writers","front":"Why were women important to the book market?","back":"Many novel readers and buyers were women.","question":"Why were women important to the book market?","answer":"Many novel readers and buyers were women.","german":"Frauen prägten den Buchmarkt als Leserinnen, Käuferinnen und Autorinnen.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c38","sourceId":38,"level":7,"category":"Women writers","front":"Why did some women use male pseudonyms?","back":"It was harder for women to get published; writing was seen as a male activity.","question":"Why did some women use male pseudonyms?","answer":"It was harder for women to get published; writing was seen as a male activity.","german":"Manche Autorinnen nutzten männliche oder geschlechtlich nicht eindeutige Namen gegen Vorurteile. Das gilt nicht für alle.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c39","sourceId":39,"level":7,"category":"Women writers","front":"Who was George Eliot?","back":"Mary Ann Evans, a writer who used a male pen name.","question":"Who was George Eliot?","answer":"Mary Ann Evans, a writer who used a male pen name.","german":"George Eliot ist das Pseudonym von Mary Ann Evans.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."},{"id":"c40","sourceId":40,"level":7,"category":"Women writers","front":"Name two other important Victorian women writers.","back":"Charlotte Brontë and Emily Brontë.","question":"Name two other important Victorian women writers.","answer":"Charlotte Brontë and Emily Brontë.","german":"Charlotte Brontë schrieb Jane Eyre; Emily Brontë schrieb Wuthering Heights.","origin":"Provided victorian_flashcards.json; front and back preserved verbatim. German explanations added for this chapter."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1-01","level":1,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"Which statement best captures the contradiction of Victorian progress?","options":["Industry and wealth grew while poverty and inequality continued.","Industrial growth immediately ended poverty.","No towns grew during Victoria’s reign.","Novels stopped discussing social problems."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Economic growth did not benefit everybody equally. This tension is useful context for literary analysis."},{"id":"q1-02","level":1,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"A reader calls Great Expectations Victorian because every event happens after 1837. What is the best correction?","options":["It is a Victorian publication, but much of the action is set earlier.","Victoria wrote it before becoming queen.","A novel’s setting must match its publication year.","It was published after the Victorian Age ended."],"correct":0,"explanation":"A period label can describe when a book was written or published, without determining its fictional setting."},{"id":"q1-03","level":1,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"Which change most directly describes urbanisation?","options":["More people live in expanding towns and cities.","A character learns to forgive a friend.","Books appear one chapter at a time.","A writer changes their name."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Urbanisation concerns settlement and population patterns, rather than narration or publication."},{"id":"q1-04","level":1,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"Which connection between context and novel is most convincing?","options":["Pip’s shame about Joe helps Dickens explore judgements based on social rank.","Pip’s new clothes prove that Victorian poverty disappeared.","London guarantees Pip’s moral improvement.","All characters express identical middle-class values."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Use a concrete character relationship to explain a social issue. Pip’s attitude, rather than his clothes alone, supports the argument."},{"id":"q1-05","level":1,"type":"tf","phase":"practise","prompt":"The Industrial Revolution began only when Victoria became queen.","correct":false,"explanation":"Industrialisation began before 1837 and continued during Victoria’s reign. The reign and the industrial process are different timelines."},{"id":"q1-06","level":1,"type":"tf","phase":"practise","prompt":"Entertainment and social criticism can exist in the same Victorian novel.","correct":true,"explanation":"An engaging story can also make readers question social arrangements. The two purposes are compatible."},{"id":"q1-07","level":1,"type":"tf","phase":"practise","prompt":"The middle class was an important reading audience, but it was not the only audience.","correct":true,"explanation":"Avoid turning an important social group into the entire reading public."},{"id":"q1-08","level":1,"type":"gap","phase":"practise","prompt":"Queen Victoria’s reign lasted from ___ to ___.","answers":[["1837"],["1901"]],"explanation":"These dates mark her reign. They do not date every scene in Great Expectations."},{"id":"q1-09","level":1,"type":"gap","phase":"practise","prompt":"The growth of towns and cities is called ___.","answers":[["urbanisation","urbanization"]],"explanation":"Urbanisation is the correct historical term; British and American spelling are accepted."},{"id":"q1-10","level":1,"type":"match","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Connect the historical change with its meaning.","pairs":[["Industrialisation","Expansion of machine-based production"],["Urbanisation","Growth of towns and cities"],["Social inequality","Unequal access to resources and opportunities"]],"explanation":"Related changes should still be defined separately. City growth is not itself a definition of social inequality."},{"id":"q1-11","level":1,"type":"sentence","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Build a clear exam sentence.","parts":["The Victorian Age","combined industrial progress","with continuing social inequality."],"explanation":"The sentence expresses a contrast rather than claiming that progress automatically ended poverty."},{"id":"q1-12","level":1,"type":"open","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Explain one way in which industrial and social change shaped Victorian fiction. Give a connection to Pip.","model":"Industrial growth and urbanisation made social differences highly visible. Novelists could entertain readers while questioning the distribution of wealth and opportunity. In Great Expectations, Pip hopes that money will make him a gentleman. His changing attitude towards Joe shows how social ambition can distort personal relationships.","criteria":["Context: explain industrial or urban change","Connection: link social class to Pip and Joe","Analysis: explain the link instead of just naming a theme"],"keywords":["Context","Connection","Analysis"]},{"id":"q1-13","level":1,"type":"open","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Explain why publication date and fictional setting should not be confused.","model":"A publication date tells us when a work entered the literary world. A setting tells us when and where its story takes place. Great Expectations appeared in 1860–1861, during the Victorian Age, although much of its action is set earlier. Its publication context still shapes how we study its social concerns.","criteria":["Distinction: separate publication from setting","Accuracy: give 1860–1861","Application: explain why the novel can still be Victorian"],"keywords":["Distinction","Accuracy","Application"]},{"id":"q1-14","level":1,"type":"open","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Is “Victorian society was simply a success story” a convincing introduction? Improve it.","model":"That statement is too simple. The Victorian Age involved industrial progress and a growing middle class, but also poverty and inequality. This contradiction helps explain why novelists examined social responsibility. A balanced introduction recognises both progress and continuing problems before linking them to a novel.","criteria":["Balance: include progress and inequality","Literature: connect the context to novels","Precision: avoid an absolute statement about everyone"],"keywords":["Balance","Literature","Precision"]},{"id":"q2-01","level":2,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"A reader borrows a novel for a subscription instead of buying it. Which institution makes this possible?","options":["A circulating library","A cliffhanger","An omniscient narrator","A pen name"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A circulating library concerns access and lending, not a narrative device."},{"id":"q2-02","level":2,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"What is the clearest effect of a long wait between instalments?","options":["A writer has a reason to leave readers curious about what comes next.","Every character must repeat the entire plot.","A story can no longer include realistic details.","The ending must be written by the readers."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The format rewards continuing interest. It does not impose one rigid structure or remove the author’s choices."},{"id":"q2-03","level":2,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"Which distinction between a periodical and an instalment is correct?","options":["A periodical is the recurring publication; an instalment is one part of a story inside it.","They are two names for a narrator.","An instalment is always a library subscription.","A periodical must contain only one complete novel."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Different terms describe the publication container and the portion of the work."},{"id":"q2-04","level":2,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"Which claim about reviewers is the most careful?","options":["Their judgements could affect a book’s reputation and its readers.","They secretly wrote all endings.","They had no place in literary culture.","They controlled every reader’s private opinion."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Reviews contribute to public discussion without controlling every individual response."},{"id":"q2-05","level":2,"type":"tf","phase":"practise","prompt":"Every Victorian serial instalment ended with a cliffhanger.","correct":false,"explanation":"Serial publication encouraged suspense, but it did not require every instalment to end with a cliffhanger."},{"id":"q2-06","level":2,"type":"tf","phase":"practise","prompt":"Great Expectations was first serialised in All the Year Round.","correct":true,"explanation":"The novel appeared there in 1860–1861. This is its publication history, not the date of every fictional event."},{"id":"q2-07","level":2,"type":"tf","phase":"practise","prompt":"A circulating library and a periodical are exactly the same thing.","correct":false,"explanation":"A circulating library lends books; a periodical is issued regularly. Both can help readers access fiction in different ways."},{"id":"q2-08","level":2,"type":"gap","phase":"practise","prompt":"A novel published in parts appears in ___.","answers":[["instalments","installments"]],"explanation":"Both British instalments and American installments are accepted."},{"id":"q2-09","level":2,"type":"gap","phase":"practise","prompt":"An unresolved ending that makes readers want the next part is a ___.","answers":[["cliffhanger","cliff-hanger"]],"explanation":"A cliffhanger is a particular technique for creating suspense; suspense is the wider effect."},{"id":"q2-10","level":2,"type":"match","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Match each publishing term with its function.","pairs":[["Circulating library","Lends books to readers"],["Review","Offers a public assessment of a work"],["Serial instalment","Continues a story one part at a time"]],"explanation":"These functions connect literary production, access and public discussion without making them interchangeable."},{"id":"q2-11","level":2,"type":"sentence","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Build a clear exam sentence.","parts":["Serial publication","encouraged writers to sustain suspense","between instalments."],"explanation":"“Encouraged” is more precise than claiming that all serials followed an identical formula."},{"id":"q2-12","level":2,"type":"open","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Explain how Magwitch’s revelation can be discussed in relation to serial storytelling.","model":"The revelation changes Pip’s understanding of his own life: his benefactor is Magwitch, not Miss Havisham. Such a turning point can create suspense and make readers reconsider earlier events. In a serial, this renewed curiosity helps sustain interest across the pauses between instalments.","criteria":["Event: identify the corrected assumption","Technique: explain suspense or re-interpretation","Publication: connect the effect to serial reading"],"keywords":["Event","Technique","Publication"]},{"id":"q2-13","level":2,"type":"open","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Describe two different ways readers could gain access to Victorian fiction.","model":"Readers could borrow books from circulating libraries, often through a subscription. They could also encounter stories in periodicals, where novels appeared in instalments. These routes made access less dependent on buying a complete novel immediately, although access still depended on circumstances and cost.","criteria":["Library: explain borrowing","Periodical: explain serial access","Nuance: avoid saying all readers had equal access"],"keywords":["Library","Periodical","Nuance"]},{"id":"q2-14","level":2,"type":"open","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Evaluate the claim that serial readers controlled Victorian novels.","model":"Readers’ responses and reviews could influence the literary market and sometimes writers’ decisions. However, influence is not the same as full control. A convincing answer explains the pressure to sustain interest while recognising that authors still made artistic choices about characters, structure and endings.","criteria":["Influence: mention reactions or reviews","Limit: distinguish pressure from control","Form: connect the discussion to serial publication"],"keywords":["Influence","Limit","Form"]},{"id":"q3-01","level":3,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"A passage shows ordinary work, money problems and social differences. Which feature does this most directly illustrate?","options":["Realism","A male pseudonym","Literary nonsense","A library subscription"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Recognisable social experience is evidence of realism, even when a larger novel also has dramatic elements."},{"id":"q3-02","level":3,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"Which statement accurately describes the narrative perspective of Great Expectations?","options":["Adult Pip retrospectively tells his own story.","An unnamed narrator directly knows all characters’ thoughts.","Estella narrates Pip’s whole life.","Joe writes the story as it happens."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The difference between older narrator and younger character is central to Pip’s reflection on his mistakes."},{"id":"q3-03","level":3,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"Which question is specifically about social criticism?","options":["How does the story challenge judging people by wealth?","How many instalments fit in a library?","Which vowels appear in Pip’s name?","Does every paragraph contain a quotation?"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Social criticism examines values, injustice and social structures rather than merely listing formal details."},{"id":"q3-04","level":3,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"Why can Gothic scenes coexist with realism?","options":["A recognisable social world can also contain symbolic, mysterious or disturbing scenes.","Realism requires every scene to be cheerful.","A Gothic scene automatically makes a book nonsense verse.","Realism forbids any moral question."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Literary features can overlap. One atmospheric technique does not cancel the social world of the novel."},{"id":"q3-05","level":3,"type":"tf","phase":"practise","prompt":"Pip is an omniscient narrator.","correct":false,"explanation":"Pip is a retrospective first-person narrator. He can reflect on his experience, but he does not directly know every character’s mind."},{"id":"q3-06","level":3,"type":"tf","phase":"practise","prompt":"Didacticism concerns a work’s capacity or intention to teach.","correct":true,"explanation":"A moral lesson can emerge through experience and consequences, without being stated as a rule in every scene."},{"id":"q3-07","level":3,"type":"tf","phase":"practise","prompt":"All Victorian novels use the same narrator and the same kind of setting.","correct":false,"explanation":"Victorian fiction is diverse. Omniscience and urban settings are useful patterns, not universal requirements."},{"id":"q3-08","level":3,"type":"gap","phase":"practise","prompt":"Criticism of unjust social values is called social ___.","answers":[["criticism"]],"explanation":"Social criticism asks what the represented world invites readers to question."},{"id":"q3-09","level":3,"type":"gap","phase":"practise","prompt":"Adult Pip tells the story using a retrospective ___ narrator.","answers":[["first-person","first person"]],"explanation":"Retrospective means looking back. First-person identifies the position of the narrator."},{"id":"q3-10","level":3,"type":"match","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Connect each feature with an example from the novel.","pairs":[["Social criticism","Pip’s treatment of Joe exposes prejudice about class"],["Didacticism","Pip learns to value kindness above status"],["First-person reflection","Adult Pip evaluates his younger self’s mistakes"]],"explanation":"A single event can support several readings. These pairings identify the emphasis of each example."},{"id":"q3-11","level":3,"type":"sentence","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Build a clear exam sentence.","parts":["Unlike an omniscient narrator,","Pip recounts his own experiences","from a retrospective first-person perspective."],"explanation":"The comparison names both the typical technique and the specific exception."},{"id":"q3-12","level":3,"type":"open","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Distinguish realism, social criticism and didacticism using Pip’s social ambition.","model":"Realism places Pip’s ambition in a recognisable world of work, money and social differences. Social criticism emerges when his behaviour exposes the unfair assumption that status measures personal worth. Didacticism concerns the moral learning that follows: Pip comes to value loyalty and kindness rather than rank alone.","criteria":["Definitions: distinguish the three terms","Example: use Pip’s ambition or Joe","Analysis: explain how the terms overlap without becoming identical"],"keywords":["Definitions","Example","Analysis"]},{"id":"q3-13","level":3,"type":"open","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Why is the difference between adult Pip and young Pip important?","model":"Young Pip experiences desire, shame and mistaken hopes without fully understanding them. Adult Pip narrates those experiences later and can judge his earlier behaviour. This distance lets readers see both the attraction of social ambition and its moral costs. It does not turn Pip into an all-knowing narrator.","criteria":["Time: distinguish narrating and younger Pip","Effect: explain reflection or irony","Limit: reject omniscience"],"keywords":["Time","Effect","Limit"]},{"id":"q3-14","level":3,"type":"open","phase":"challenge","prompt":"A student says Gothic atmosphere disproves realism. Explain why that is too rigid.","model":"Realism and Gothic atmosphere concern different aspects of a novel. Dickens can represent class relations and everyday work convincingly while using Miss Havisham’s disturbing house to create mystery and decay. The atmosphere can deepen social and psychological meaning rather than removing the story from a recognisable world.","criteria":["Distinction: separate social representation from atmosphere","Example: use Miss Havisham’s house","Nuance: explain how features can coexist"],"keywords":["Distinction","Example","Nuance"]},{"id":"q4-01","level":4,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"A novel studies status, social conventions and outward respectability. Which category is the best starting point?","options":["Novel of manners","A dictionary of industrial terms","A periodical review","A grammar workbook"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A novel of manners focuses on social behaviour and conventions. This does not prevent it from having other features."},{"id":"q4-02","level":4,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"Which pairing of author and work is correct?","options":["Charlotte Brontë — Jane Eyre","Emily Brontë — Vanity Fair","Thackeray — A Book of Nonsense","Edward Lear — Great Expectations"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Jane Eyre is by Charlotte Brontë and is a useful example of a narrative of formation."},{"id":"q4-03","level":4,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"Why is a strict choice between “Bildungsroman” and “humanitarian novel” misleading for Great Expectations?","options":["Pip’s development and the novel’s compassion for disadvantaged people work together.","A novel cannot contain a main character and social issues.","Both terms mean that a book is published weekly.","Dickens wrote only nonsense verse."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Categories can describe different dimensions of the same text."},{"id":"q4-04","level":4,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"Which statement avoids a mistake about Edward Lear?","options":["He is a major nonsense writer, especially known for verse.","He wrote Jane Eyre as a social realist novel.","Every nonsense work must be a long novel.","Nonsense means the author had no control over language."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The category is broader than the novel. Its playful effects are deliberate."},{"id":"q4-05","level":4,"type":"tf","phase":"practise","prompt":"A Bildungsroman shows only physical ageing, not a change in understanding.","correct":false,"explanation":"A Bildungsroman concerns formation, identity and development. Inner change matters, not merely the passing of years."},{"id":"q4-06","level":4,"type":"tf","phase":"practise","prompt":"Lewis Carroll’s Alice’s Adventures in Wonderland is associated with literary nonsense.","correct":true,"explanation":"Its playful challenges to logic and language make it a central example of the category."},{"id":"q4-07","level":4,"type":"tf","phase":"practise","prompt":"The four teaching categories must never overlap.","correct":false,"explanation":"A work can have several features. Great Expectations links formation with humanitarian concerns."},{"id":"q4-08","level":4,"type":"gap","phase":"practise","prompt":"A novel of formation is also called a ___.","answers":[["bildungsroman","novel of formation"]],"explanation":"The German term is widely used in English literary analysis."},{"id":"q4-09","level":4,"type":"gap","phase":"practise","prompt":"Thackeray’s ___ Fair is a useful example of a novel of manners.","answers":[["vanity"]],"explanation":"Use the work as evidence of social observation rather than assuming a category defines every page."},{"id":"q4-10","level":4,"type":"match","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Match the work with its author.","pairs":[["Vanity Fair","William Makepeace Thackeray"],["Jane Eyre","Charlotte Brontë"],["A Book of Nonsense","Edward Lear"]],"explanation":"These author-work pairings are distinct; the wider categories may overlap."},{"id":"q4-11","level":4,"type":"sentence","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Build a clear exam sentence.","parts":["Great Expectations combines","the development of a young person","with humanitarian social criticism."],"explanation":"This sentence explains the compatibility of two categories instead of treating labels as competing answers."},{"id":"q4-12","level":4,"type":"open","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Explain why growing older alone does not make a story a Bildungsroman.","model":"A Bildungsroman shows the formation of identity and understanding through experience. A character can grow older without changing their values. Pip’s story matters because his idea of a gentleman changes: he learns that kindness and loyalty are more important than wealth. This inner development supports the category.","criteria":["Definition: explain formation","Distinction: separate ageing and learning","Evidence: use Pip’s changing values"],"keywords":["Definition","Distinction","Evidence"]},{"id":"q4-13","level":4,"type":"open","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Compare a novel of manners with a humanitarian novel.","model":"A novel of manners examines the codes and behaviour of a social world, while a humanitarian novel emphasises sympathy and injustice. The interests can overlap. A text may expose conventions of respectability while showing how they harm vulnerable people. Explain the emphasis rather than assuming the two categories exclude each other.","criteria":["Manners: define social conventions","Humanitarian: define compassion and injustice","Comparison: identify possible overlap"],"keywords":["Manners","Humanitarian","Comparison"]},{"id":"q4-14","level":4,"type":"open","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Explain why the heading “four types of Victorian novels” needs qualification when it includes Lear.","model":"The heading simplifies a wider literary landscape for teaching. Literary nonsense includes prose and poetry, and Lear is especially associated with nonsense verse. It is therefore more precise to call these categories of Victorian fiction and writing, rather than suggesting that every representative wrote the same kind of novel.","criteria":["Scope: identify nonsense as a broader category","Example: mention Lear’s verse","Precision: propose a more accurate description"],"keywords":["Scope","Example","Precision"]},{"id":"q5-01","level":5,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"Which relationship most clearly exposes the difference between social manners and loyalty?","options":["Pip’s embarrassment about the kind Joe","A reviewer lending a periodical","Lear writing nonsense verse","Victoria subscribing to a library"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Joe’s loyalty offers a concrete contrast to Pip’s desire for social polish."},{"id":"q5-02","level":5,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"What is the most careful claim about Magwitch?","options":["His role makes social labels an unreliable guide to a person’s full moral value.","His money proves that wealth always creates moral perfection.","His conviction makes every form of kindness impossible.","He is Estella’s cruel husband in the supplied summary."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The interpretation challenges prejudice without claiming that any character is morally flawless."},{"id":"q5-03","level":5,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"Which connection should not be confused with financial support?","options":["Pip’s romantic feelings for Estella","Magwitch’s role as benefactor","Money for Pip’s new life","The funding of Pip’s London education"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A romantic hope does not establish the source of the money. Pip’s assumptions about these connections are mistaken."},{"id":"q5-04","level":5,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"How can Miss Havisham be used in a discussion of appearances?","options":["Her wealth helps make Pip’s mistaken belief about his benefactor seem plausible to him.","Her status proves that she tells the story as Pip.","Her wealth means all characters understand her intentions correctly.","Her home removes every element of mystery."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Pip interprets social appearances, but his conclusion is not proof of the underlying reality."},{"id":"q5-05","level":5,"type":"tf","phase":"practise","prompt":"Joe is Pip’s biological father.","correct":false,"explanation":"Joe is married to Pip’s sister and is therefore Pip’s brother-in-law. His caring role should not be confused with biological parenthood."},{"id":"q5-06","level":5,"type":"tf","phase":"practise","prompt":"Magwitch is Pip’s secret benefactor.","correct":true,"explanation":"This discovery changes Pip’s understanding of his support and challenges his earlier assumptions."},{"id":"q5-07","level":5,"type":"tf","phase":"practise","prompt":"Dickens presents money as a reliable measurement of every character’s moral value.","correct":false,"explanation":"The novel questions this measurement. Kindness and loyalty do not depend on rank or wealth."},{"id":"q5-08","level":5,"type":"gap","phase":"practise","prompt":"Joe represents kindness, honesty and ___.","answers":[["loyalty"]],"explanation":"Loyalty concerns continued support, not the ability to appear fashionable."},{"id":"q5-09","level":5,"type":"gap","phase":"practise","prompt":"Pip’s real benefactor is ___, rather than Miss Havisham.","answers":[["magwitch","abel magwitch"]],"explanation":"The revelation links an early encounter with the hidden source of Pip’s later support."},{"id":"q5-10","level":5,"type":"match","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Match each connection with the correct description.","pairs":[["Miss Havisham and Estella","Adoptive mother and daughter"],["Pip and Joe","A young relative and his loyal brother-in-law"],["Magwitch and Pip","Hidden benefactor and recipient of support"]],"explanation":"Family, emotional bonds and financial support are different types of relationship."},{"id":"q5-11","level":5,"type":"sentence","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Build a clear exam sentence.","parts":["Joe’s loyalty","shows that moral worth","does not depend on social status."],"explanation":"The verb “shows” must lead to an interpretation, not merely repeat Joe’s occupation."},{"id":"q5-12","level":5,"type":"open","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Explain Joe’s function without merely listing his qualities.","model":"Joe’s kindness and loyalty form a contrast to Pip’s desire for refined manners. When Pip feels embarrassed by him, the reader sees how social ambition can damage gratitude and affection. Joe therefore helps Dickens question the assumption that a gentleman’s appearance makes someone morally superior.","criteria":["Quality: identify kindness or loyalty","Relationship: explain Pip’s embarrassment","Function: connect the contrast to class criticism"],"keywords":["Quality","Relationship","Function"]},{"id":"q5-13","level":5,"type":"open","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Distinguish three relationships around Estella and explain why the distinctions matter.","model":"Estella is Miss Havisham’s adopted daughter, Pip is in love with her, and she marries Bentley Drummle. These are different connections. Pip’s romantic hopes do not make him her husband, and they do not prove that Miss Havisham funds his life. Keeping the relationships separate prevents Pip’s assumptions from becoming facts.","criteria":["Family: name adoption","Romance and marriage: distinguish Pip and Drummle","Analysis: separate hopes from financial evidence"],"keywords":["Family","Romance and marriage","Analysis"]},{"id":"q5-14","level":5,"type":"open","phase":"challenge","prompt":"How does Magwitch complicate an easy opposition between “respectable” and “bad”?","model":"Magwitch’s public identity as a convict makes him an object of fear and prejudice. His role as Pip’s benefactor reveals care and commitment that this label alone does not explain. Dickens asks readers to judge actions and relationships, not merely social reputation. This is a humanitarian challenge to a simple moral hierarchy.","criteria":["Label: identify conviction or reputation","Counter-evidence: explain his support","Interpretation: connect the complication to prejudice"],"keywords":["Label","Counter-evidence","Interpretation"]},{"id":"q6-01","level":6,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"Which answer actually explains the label Bildungsroman?","options":["Pip revises his values through experience and regret.","Pip appears in many chapters.","London is a real city.","The book has a Victorian author."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Formation requires an account of development, not merely length, geography or authorship."},{"id":"q6-02","level":6,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"Which example most directly supports an interpretation of Gothic atmosphere?","options":["Miss Havisham’s decaying, unsettling home","A list of library subscription fees","The date of Victoria’s accession","A dictionary entry for realism"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The home’s atmosphere and decay provide textual material for a Gothic reading."},{"id":"q6-03","level":6,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"Which rural–urban comparison is most defensible?","options":["The contrast helps examine Pip’s ambition and values without making either place uniformly good or bad.","Every rural character is morally perfect.","London automatically removes class prejudice.","The forge and London have identical functions."],"correct":0,"explanation":"A comparison should explain a pattern while remaining open to complexity."},{"id":"q6-04","level":6,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"Which sentence best moves from evidence to interpretation?","options":["Joe remains loyal, so Pip’s shame exposes the limits of judging people by social polish.","Joe is a blacksmith, and then Pip is in the story.","Dickens is famous, so every statement about the novel is true.","The novel has characters and therefore teaches nothing."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The sentence explains how a relationship supports an argument about class and moral value."},{"id":"q6-05","level":6,"type":"tf","phase":"practise","prompt":"An interpretation becomes convincing simply by naming several literary terms.","correct":false,"explanation":"A convincing interpretation defines the relevant idea, selects evidence and explains the connection. Labels alone are not analysis."},{"id":"q6-06","level":6,"type":"tf","phase":"practise","prompt":"Retrospective narration can create distance between young Pip’s beliefs and the older narrator’s understanding.","correct":true,"explanation":"The narrator’s later perspective makes reflection on earlier mistakes possible."},{"id":"q6-07","level":6,"type":"tf","phase":"practise","prompt":"London is presented as a guarantee that Pip will become morally better.","correct":false,"explanation":"London offers opportunities and pressures. Pip’s moral learning cannot be reduced to a change of address."},{"id":"q6-08","level":6,"type":"gap","phase":"practise","prompt":"A clear analysis moves from claim to evidence to ___.","answers":[["explanation","analysis","interpretation"]],"explanation":"The final step makes the evidence relevant to the argument."},{"id":"q6-09","level":6,"type":"gap","phase":"practise","prompt":"Mystery, decay and a disturbing atmosphere can be described as ___ elements.","answers":[["gothic"]],"explanation":"Gothic elements can coexist with realism and social criticism."},{"id":"q6-10","level":6,"type":"match","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Connect a literary claim with a suitable example.","pairs":[["Moral development","Pip rethinks his shame about his background"],["Gothic atmosphere","Miss Havisham’s unsettling, decaying house"],["Humanitarian concern","A convicted man is more than his social label"]],"explanation":"The example must support the claim’s specific focus. Do not treat every detail as evidence for every term."},{"id":"q6-11","level":6,"type":"sentence","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Build a clear exam sentence.","parts":["By contrasting social ambition with loyalty,","Dickens questions the idea","that wealth determines human worth."],"explanation":"The opening phrase identifies a technique; the rest explains its critical purpose."},{"id":"q6-12","level":6,"type":"open","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Why is Great Expectations both a Bildungsroman and a humanitarian novel?","model":"It is a Bildungsroman because Pip’s experiences change his identity and moral understanding. It is also humanitarian because Joe’s loyalty and Magwitch’s support challenge easy judgements based on class and reputation. The two dimensions work together: Pip’s growth requires him to recognise the humanity of people he has misunderstood.","criteria":["Formation: identify inner change","Humanitarian: explain compassion or prejudice","Connection: show how both categories work together"],"keywords":["Formation","Humanitarian","Connection"]},{"id":"q6-13","level":6,"type":"open","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Explain a possible effect of Miss Havisham’s Gothic home. Avoid treating your reading as the only possible one.","model":"The decay and unsettling atmosphere of Miss Havisham’s home can suggest a life trapped in the past. This makes the house more than a background for events. It can contrast with Pip’s expectations of a successful future and connect external space with emotional damage. This is an interpretation supported by the setting’s features.","criteria":["Features: identify decay, mystery or unease","Effect: explain an interpretive possibility","Precision: distinguish interpretation from a bare fact"],"keywords":["Features","Effect","Precision"]},{"id":"q6-14","level":6,"type":"open","phase":"challenge","prompt":"How does first-person narration strengthen the criticism of Pip’s class attitudes?","model":"Readers follow social ambition through the feelings of the younger Pip, while the older narrator can reflect on his mistakes. This double perspective allows the attraction of status to be understood without simply endorsing it. Pip’s embarrassment about Joe becomes evidence of a faulty judgement that the later voice can reconsider.","criteria":["Perspective: name retrospective first person","Evidence: include Pip and Joe","Effect: explain critical distance or moral reflection"],"keywords":["Perspective","Evidence","Effect"]},{"id":"q7-01","level":7,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"Which author-work pairing is accurate?","options":["Emily Brontë — Wuthering Heights","Charlotte Brontë — Middlemarch","George Eliot — Great Expectations","Edward Lear — Jane Eyre"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The sisters and Eliot should be identified individually rather than treated as interchangeable names."},{"id":"q7-02","level":7,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"A book is published under a name different from its author’s usual name. Which term describes this?","options":["Pseudonym","Instalment","Urbanisation","Omniscience"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A pseudonym describes authorship and naming, not narrative knowledge or serial form."},{"id":"q7-03","level":7,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"Which account of women in literary culture is most balanced?","options":["They helped shape fiction as writers and readers while facing unequal opportunities.","They were only readers and never published.","Every woman used the same male name.","No woman faced professional prejudice."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Literary achievement and structural barriers can coexist. Avoid both erasure and the claim that equality was already complete."},{"id":"q7-04","level":7,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"What is the best explanation of the Bell names used by the Brontës?","options":["They did not openly identify the writers as women.","They prove the novels were actually written by three men.","They identify three different circulating libraries.","They are the names of Pip’s benefactors."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Currer, Ellis and Acton Bell were publication names. Gender presentation and identity are not the same thing."},{"id":"q7-05","level":7,"type":"tf","phase":"practise","prompt":"George Eliot was Mary Ann Evans’s literary name.","correct":true,"explanation":"She is an important woman novelist, despite the masculine form of her pen name."},{"id":"q7-06","level":7,"type":"tf","phase":"practise","prompt":"Every Victorian woman writer had to publish under a male pseudonym.","correct":false,"explanation":"Some used male or gender-ambiguous names; others published under their own names. Strategies and experiences varied."},{"id":"q7-07","level":7,"type":"tf","phase":"practise","prompt":"Women’s role in Victorian literature was limited to buying novels written by men.","correct":false,"explanation":"Women were major authors as well as readers and purchasers, including the Brontës and George Eliot."},{"id":"q7-08","level":7,"type":"gap","phase":"practise","prompt":"George Eliot was the pen name of Mary Ann ___.","answers":[["evans"]],"explanation":"Mary Ann Evans is the form used in the lesson sheet; Mary Anne and Marian are also found in biographical accounts."},{"id":"q7-09","level":7,"type":"gap","phase":"practise","prompt":"A name used for publication instead of the writer’s usual name is a ___.","answers":[["pseudonym","pen name"]],"explanation":"The definition does not imply that every pseudonym is masculine."},{"id":"q7-10","level":7,"type":"match","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Connect the writer with the work.","pairs":[["Charlotte Brontë","Jane Eyre"],["Emily Brontë","Wuthering Heights"],["George Eliot","Middlemarch"]],"explanation":"Keep these pairings distinct when using authors as examples in an exam answer."},{"id":"q7-11","level":7,"type":"sentence","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Build a clear exam sentence.","parts":["Women shaped Victorian fiction","both as readers and as writers,","despite social and professional restrictions."],"explanation":"The sentence recognises agency and achievement without denying barriers."},{"id":"q7-12","level":7,"type":"open","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Explain why “women were not allowed to write” is an inadequate summary of this topic.","model":"That claim erases the work of successful writers such as Charlotte and Emily Brontë and George Eliot. Women faced prejudice and restrictions, but they still shaped the literary market and its ideas. A better answer distinguishes unequal opportunities from an absolute absence of female authorship.","criteria":["Examples: name at least two writers","Barriers: recognise prejudice or restrictions","Nuance: distinguish inequality from total exclusion"],"keywords":["Examples","Barriers","Nuance"]},{"id":"q7-13","level":7,"type":"open","phase":"challenge","prompt":"How can a pen name reveal a problem in the literary market?","model":"A male or gender-ambiguous name could reduce the chance that readers or reviewers judged a work through assumptions about women. This suggests that recognition was not based only on the text. However, the strategy does not prove that all women used pseudonyms or that every writer had exactly the same motives.","criteria":["Definition: explain the naming strategy","Analysis: connect it to reader or reviewer expectations","Limit: avoid universal motives"],"keywords":["Definition","Analysis","Limit"]},{"id":"q7-14","level":7,"type":"open","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Connect women’s authorship with the broader reader–writer relationship of the age.","model":"Women were an important part of the reading public and also produced influential fiction. Their work helped shape what novels could discuss, including identity, morality and social expectations. A literary market with a growing audience created opportunities, but gender prejudice could still affect access to publication and recognition.","criteria":["Agency: include women as writers and readers","Market: connect audience and publication","Tension: mention opportunity alongside restriction"],"keywords":["Agency","Market","Tension"]},{"id":"q8-01","level":8,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"An exam asks how publication influenced form. Which plan answers the actual task?","options":["Define instalments, explain suspense, and connect a turning point to continuing reader interest.","List every character’s name without mentioning publication.","Describe only Victoria’s clothes.","Claim that every Victorian text has an omniscient narrator."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The task asks for a relationship between market form and storytelling, not a general list of facts."},{"id":"q8-02","level":8,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"Which thesis best combines a pattern with a relevant exception?","options":["The novel shares Victorian social concerns but uses Pip’s retrospective first-person voice rather than an omniscient narrator.","The book is Victorian, so every character must be all-knowing.","Pip is first-person, so no social criticism is possible.","All Victorian books are nonsense poems."],"correct":0,"explanation":"A nuanced thesis can describe representative concerns without forcing a text to fit every general feature."},{"id":"q8-03","level":8,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"Which piece of evidence best supports a paragraph about class prejudice?","options":["Pip feels ashamed of Joe although Joe remains loyal.","The novel’s title contains two words.","The model answer uses a full stop.","A periodical appears regularly."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The evidence must address the specific argument. A fact can be true yet irrelevant to a particular paragraph."},{"id":"q8-04","level":8,"type":"mc","phase":"practise","prompt":"Which revision most improves “The house is Gothic because it is Gothic”?","options":["Its decay, secrets and unsettling atmosphere create unease and can suggest a life trapped in the past.","Gothic is a long word and is therefore correct.","Every house in London is Gothic.","The author was Victorian, which explains every detail."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Replace a circular label with observable features and an explanation of their possible effect."},{"id":"q8-05","level":8,"type":"tf","phase":"practise","prompt":"An accurate event can support different interpretations if the connection is explained.","correct":true,"explanation":"Evidence is not a label with only one use. The reasoning must make clear which aspect supports the claim."},{"id":"q8-06","level":8,"type":"tf","phase":"practise","prompt":"A model answer automatically proves that an unmarked free-text answer is correct.","correct":false,"explanation":"Free writing needs comparison and judgement. This site does not claim to grade complex answers automatically."},{"id":"q8-07","level":8,"type":"tf","phase":"practise","prompt":"A strong answer can simply copy a list of terms without explaining them.","correct":false,"explanation":"A strong answer defines relevant ideas and links them to examples. Lists alone do not demonstrate analysis."},{"id":"q8-08","level":8,"type":"gap","phase":"practise","prompt":"Unlike many Victorian novels, Great Expectations uses a ___ narrator.","answers":[["first-person","first person","retrospective first-person","retrospective first person"]],"explanation":"Mentioning this exception improves accuracy; do not call Pip omniscient."},{"id":"q8-09","level":8,"type":"gap","phase":"practise","prompt":"The novel questions whether social status determines moral ___.","answers":[["worth","value"]],"explanation":"Moral worth or value is the concept being contrasted with social rank."},{"id":"q8-10","level":8,"type":"match","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Choose evidence that directly supports each exam claim.","pairs":[["Publication shapes storytelling","Instalments encourage suspense between parts"],["Moral growth matters","Pip reconsiders his earlier shame"],["Gender affected recognition","Some women used male or ambiguous pen names"]],"explanation":"Each claim needs evidence at the same level: publication, character development or the literary market."},{"id":"q8-11","level":8,"type":"sentence","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Build a clear exam sentence.","parts":["Although the novel shares Victorian social concerns,","its retrospective first-person narrator","offers a specific perspective on Pip’s moral development."],"explanation":"The concessive opening lets the sentence combine a general context with a distinctive narrative technique."},{"id":"q8-12","level":8,"type":"open","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Write 120–150 words: Why is Great Expectations a typical Victorian novel? Use at least three features and acknowledge one exception.","model":"Great Expectations is a Victorian novel because it connects individual experience with wider social questions. Pip’s ambition reflects a society that often links success to wealth and rank. His shame about Joe exposes the cruelty of judging people by manners rather than loyalty. Magwitch’s role as benefactor challenges prejudice against a convicted man. The novel is also a Bildungsroman: Pip grows from childhood towards a more mature understanding of himself. Its humanitarian concerns encourage sympathy for people whom respectable society may dismiss. Dickens combines a recognisable social world with Gothic elements in Miss Havisham’s decaying house. Serial publication also helps explain the importance of suspense and revelations. However, the narrative perspective is a significant qualification: adult Pip tells his own story retrospectively, rather than speaking as an omniscient narrator. His reflection makes moral development visible.","criteria":["Coverage: connect at least three literary or historical features","Evidence: explain specific examples involving Pip, Joe or Magwitch","Narrator: identify the first-person exception","Argument: link features and examples instead of listing labels","Language: aim for 120–150 coherent English words"],"keywords":["Coverage","Evidence","Narrator","Argument","Language"]},{"id":"q8-13","level":8,"type":"open","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Write a short comparative answer: how do the publishing market and social class each influence a reading of Great Expectations?","model":"Serial publication helps explain the novel’s suspense and turning points: readers need a reason to return. Social class helps explain Pip’s expectations and his shame about Joe. These are different levels of analysis, but they interact. A compelling revelation, such as the identity of the benefactor, both sustains curiosity and makes readers reconsider social prejudice.","criteria":["Publication: explain serial reader interest","Class: explain ambition or shame","Synthesis: connect revelation and social judgement"],"keywords":["Publication","Class","Synthesis"]},{"id":"q8-14","level":8,"type":"open","phase":"challenge","prompt":"Prepare a 90-second oral answer linking women writers, literary categories and the limits of generalisations.","model":"Women helped shape Victorian fiction as authors and readers. Charlotte Brontë’s Jane Eyre can be discussed as a novel of formation, while George Eliot’s work offers important examples of realism. Their contribution shows why the period cannot be described as a literature written only by men. Some women negotiated prejudice through pen names, but no single strategy or literary category describes every writer.","criteria":["Writers: give accurate author-work examples","Category: define at least one literary feature","Nuance: avoid claims about all women or all novels"],"keywords":["Writers","Category","Nuance"]}],"memory":[{"id":"terms","title":"Literary term — definition","pairs":[["Realism","A recognisable social world"],["Didacticism","An intention to teach"],["Omniscient narrator","A voice with knowledge beyond one character"],["Bildungsroman","A narrative of formation"],["Cliffhanger","An important situation left unresolved"],["Social criticism","Questioning injustice and harmful values"]]},{"id":"authors","title":"Author / work — literary emphasis","pairs":[["Thackeray: Vanity Fair","Social conventions in a novel of manners"],["Dickens: humanitarian fiction","Sympathy for vulnerable people"],["Charlotte Brontë: Jane Eyre","Formation of an individual’s identity"],["Carroll: Alice’s Adventures in Wonderland","Nonsense and disrupted logic in prose"],["Lear: A Book of Nonsense","Playful nonsense verse"]]},{"id":"characters","title":"Character — analytical function","pairs":[["Pip","Ambition followed by moral reflection"],["Joe","Loyalty beyond social polish"],["Estella","Romantic desire entangled with class shame"],["Miss Havisham","Wealth and a disturbing relation to the past"],["Magwitch","A benefactor who challenges prejudice against a convict"]]},{"id":"features","title":"Literary feature — novel example","pairs":[["Bildungsroman","Pip’s values change through experience"],["Humanitarian concern","Magwitch is not reduced to his public label"],["Gothic atmosphere","The unsettling decay of Miss Havisham’s house"],["Class criticism","Pip feels embarrassed by the loyal Joe"],["Retrospective first person","Adult Pip evaluates his younger self"]]},{"id":"translations","title":"English term — German meaning","pairs":[["Instalment","Fortsetzungsteil"],["Urbanisation","Verstädterung"],["Social inequality","Soziale Ungleichheit"],["Pen name","Pseudonym"],["Loyalty","Treue / Loyalität"],["Moral development","Moralische Entwicklung"]]}],"toolbox":{"phrases":[["The Victorian Age was characterised by ...","Die viktorianische Epoche war gekennzeichnet durch ..."],["Victorian novelists often focused on ...","Viktorianische Romanautoren konzentrierten sich häufig auf ..."],["One of the main features of Victorian literature is ...","Eines der Hauptmerkmale viktorianischer Literatur ist ..."],["Dickens criticises the social inequalities of his time.","Dickens kritisiert die sozialen Ungleichheiten seiner Zeit."],["The novel reflects the values and problems of Victorian society.","Der Roman spiegelt Werte und Probleme der viktorianischen Gesellschaft."],["Great Expectations can be considered a Bildungsroman because ...","Great Expectations kann als Bildungsroman gelten, weil ..."],["Pip undergoes a process of moral development.","Pip durchläuft eine moralische Entwicklung."],["Dickens uses Pip’s experiences to show that ...","Dickens nutzt Pips Erfahrungen, um zu zeigen, dass ..."],["Joe represents kindness, honesty and loyalty.","Joe verkörpert Güte, Ehrlichkeit und Loyalität."],["Magwitch’s role challenges conventional social prejudices.","Magwitchs Rolle stellt verbreitete soziale Vorurteile infrage."],["Unlike many Victorian novels, Great Expectations is told by a first-person narrator.","Anders als viele viktorianische Romane wird Great Expectations von einem Ich-Erzähler erzählt."],["The novel suggests that moral values are more important than wealth and social status.","Der Roman legt nahe, dass moralische Werte wichtiger sind als Reichtum und sozialer Status."],["This example supports the argument because ...","Dieses Beispiel stützt das Argument, weil ..."],["The setting can be read as a reflection of ...","Der Handlungsort lässt sich als Spiegel von ... deuten."],["However, this pattern is not a universal rule.","Dieses Muster ist jedoch keine ausnahmslose Regel."]],"model":"Great Expectations is a Victorian novel because it connects individual experience with wider social questions. Pip’s ambition reflects a society that often links success to wealth and rank. His shame about Joe exposes the cruelty of judging people by manners rather than loyalty. Magwitch’s role as benefactor challenges prejudice against a convicted man. The novel is also a Bildungsroman: Pip grows from childhood towards a more mature understanding of himself. Its humanitarian concerns encourage sympathy for people whom respectable society may dismiss. Dickens combines a recognisable social world with Gothic elements in Miss Havisham’s decaying house. Serial publication also helps explain the importance of suspense and revelations. However, the narrative perspective is a significant qualification: adult Pip tells his own story retrospectively, rather than speaking as an omniscient narrator. His reflection makes moral development visible.","sources":[{"title":"Provided lesson sheet: Victorian_Novel_Great_Expectations.docx","note":"Primary teaching basis, supplied by the user. All 40 original cards are preserved from victorian_flashcards.json; the TSV was checked for consistency. German notes and the 112 exercises are added teaching material. No textbook photographs or exact abridged novel edition were supplied."},{"title":"New College, Oxford — Victorian literature","url":"https://www.new.ox.ac.uk/english-literature-through-the-ages/victorian-1837-1900","note":"Further reading on publishing, social context and women’s authorship."},{"title":"Duke University Libraries — Poets and Novelists","url":"https://exhibits.library.duke.edu/exhibits/show/mypen/poets_novelists","note":"Author-work checks and the distinction between male and gender-ambiguous pen names."},{"title":"UCSC Dickens Project — Chronology","url":"https://dickens.ucsc.edu/resources/dickens-chronology/","note":"Checks the 1860–1861 serial publication of Great Expectations."},{"title":"Open University — Edward Lear’s nonsense songs","url":"https://www.open.edu/openlearn/history-the-arts/culture/literature-and-creative-writing/edward-lears-nonsense-songs","note":"Confirms that literary nonsense includes verse, not only novels."},{"title":"Wisbech & Fenland Museum — Great Expectations","url":"https://www.wisbechmuseum.org.uk/dickens/","note":"Museum holding the manuscript; publication and background context."}]}};
